Graduate-Level Scope
Prerequisites
The reader should be able to work with sets, functions, relations, finite strings, countable sets,
induction on natural numbers, and ordinary rigorous proofs. Prior exposure to elementary logic
is useful but not required. Chapters that use cardinal arithmetic, algebra, or topology state the
additional facts they require.
Learning outcomes
A reader completing the reference should be able to:
1. define a formal language by an inductive grammar and prove results by structural induction;
2. define capture-avoiding substitution and prove a substitution lemma;
3. prove soundness for a natural-deduction or sequent calculus;
4. prove completeness for classical propositional logic and explain a Henkin proof of completeness
for classical first-order logic;
5. derive compactness and the downward and upward Löwenheim–Skolem theorems with their
cardinal hypotheses;
6. use back-and-forth arguments, elementary chains, and ultraproducts;
7. prove undecidability by a many-one reduction;
8. state and apply the diagonal lemma and derive the first incompleteness theorem, the second
incompleteness theorem, and undefinability of truth with exact hypotheses;
9. explain cut elimination, normalization, and the subformula property;
10. give relational semantics for intuitionistic and normal modal logic and prove representative
soundness results;
11. distinguish full semantics from Henkin semantics for second-order logic; and
12. audit a written or mechanized proof for hidden assumptions, variable capture, rule misuse, and
shifts between object language and metalanguage.
